{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "Peter's Dating Misadventures", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "1000 Apologies", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 7, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 8, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B1.3", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "  Describe complicated or unusual events in the past through a wider range of B1 language in both work and social contexts.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": " Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": " Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": " Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": " Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": " Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": " Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": " Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": " Understands important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations about the communicative functions related to this level. ", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"content": "engenn8le08ob07re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": " Simple Past vs Past Perfect", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the Past Perfect tense to identify and talk about an 'earlier past'.<br />  You use the Simple Past to talk about a finished action that happened at a specific time in the past. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "When you are talking about a past event, you can refer to something that happened at an earlier time to that past event by using the <span class=\"underline\">Past Perfect </span>tense.<br /><br />  The structure of the Past Perfect is:<br /><br />  Subject + had + past participle<br /><br />  <span class=\"italic\"> I <span class=\"strong\">had wanted</span>to check it out before you decided on our date tonight.</span><br /> Which event happened <span class=\"italic\">first</span>? I wanted to check out the place.<br /> Which event happened <span class=\"italic\">later</span>? You decided on our date.<br /><br />  You use the Past Perfect tense (not Simple Past) when you want to talk about how long something had continued <span class=\"strong\">up to a past moment</span>. <br /> Example:<br /> <span class=\"italic\">They had started to win all their games until one of their players broke his ankle.</span><br /> You use the Simple Past tense to talk about the action that happened after that prior action in the Past Perfect tense.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I had only seen sports fans on TV until I met you.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "They had won more World Series than any other team until the New York Yankees took that title from them.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": " Modals for Possibility and Certainty ", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You can use modals to talk about degrees of certainty.<br /> You use them to describe what is definite, probable, possible, or impossible from the speaker's point of view. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You can use <span class=\"italic\">must</span>, <span class=\"italic\">may</span>, <span class=\"italic\">might</span>, and <span class=\"italic\">could</span> to describe degrees of certainty.<br /> You can use modals with the Present Perfect to talk about the possibility of a past event that is not clear in the present context.<br /> Example:<br /> <span class=\"italic\">She <span class=\"strong\">must have found</span> the situation frustrating</span>.<br /><br />  Must = it's very likely <br /> Must not / Could not / Can't = it's not possible<br /> May / Might / Can / Could = it's possible<br /> May not / Might not = it's possible this is not true", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":5,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"><span class=\\\"strong\\\"> Modal </span> </div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"><span class=\\\"strong\\\"> Meaning </span> </div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\"> You must be really proud of your team. </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" It's very likely that you are proud of your team.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\"> That can't be a good thing for the team! </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" It's not possible that this is a good thing for the team.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\"> We could go there next Friday.  </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" It's possible that we could go there Friday.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\"> He might not come back until next year. </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" It's not likely that he can come back until next year.\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}", "type": "table"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "enthusiastic ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "extremely happy about something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example:  I didn't know you were so enthusiastic about baseball.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le08ob07aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": " These are words you hear in a conversation about attending a baseball game.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "underdog ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a competitor or team with little chance to win a contest", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I always root for the underdog.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le08ob07aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to root for", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to take sides with, to align oneself with or show strong sympathy for someone or something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I always root for the Red Sox.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn8le08ob07aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "intimate ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "used to refer to a private space or situation ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: 